{"parentUuid":"8a153c5a-d269-4109-b57b-87eb0bbe941c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d3c558b8-5926-493c-a40f-61e97a9f4b9c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CBEcue2JufZs9eJ3vsdct2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Experience Memory Layering Design\n2\t\n3\t## Summary\n4\t\n5\tThis document maps how to extend the current memory-loop system from lesson memory toward a more MIA-like manager without losing the advantages of the current rollout design.\n6\t\n7\tIt covers two paths:\n8\t\n9\t- a beta-compatible layered design that preserves the existing lesson-memory hot path and adds experience memory as a second retrieval layer\n10\t- a longer-term experience-first redesign where episodes and playbooks become the primary memory unit and lessons become a derived product\n11\t\n12\tRecommendation:\n13\t\n14\t- ship the layered design first\n15\t- keep lesson memory as the default hot path\n16\t- add experience memory as procedural fallback\n17\t- treat the experience-first design as post-beta architecture\n18\t\n19\t## Context\n20\t\n21\tThe current memory-loop rollout is designed as a compact guidance system:\n22\t\n23\t- fetch `CheckpointAnalysis`\n24\t- generate structured memories\n25\t- store and reconcile them\n26\t- score them\n27\t- inject the top matched memories into future sessions\n28\t\n29\tThat is valuable, but it is mostly \"remember the lesson.\"\n30\t\n31\tMIA's manager is closer to \"remember the way the task was solved.\" It stores compressed task-solving experience and retrieves it to help planning and execution. The gap is not that we lack memory entirely. The gap is that our memory is mostly declarative and cautionary, not procedural.\n32\t\n33\t## Goals\n34\t\n35\t- Preserve the current lesson-memory strengths: compactness, explainability, and fast injection.\n36\t- Add procedural memory without blowing up token costs or retrieval latency.\n37\t- Keep the beta rollout path incremental and shippable in small PRs.\n38\t- Create a long-term architecture that can evolve toward planner-facing memory instead of prompt-only guidance.\n39\t\n40\t## Non-Goals\n41\t\n42\t- No attempt to reproduce MIA's reinforcement-learning setup.\n43\t- No attempt to store full raw transcripts as the primary retrieval unit.\n44\t- No beta requirement for a distinct planner service or separate execution runtime.\n45\t- No immediate replacement of the current lesson-memory rollout.\n46\t\n47\t## Current Architecture vs MIA Manager\n48\t\n49\t### Current memory loop\n50\t\n51\tPrimary unit:\n52\t\n53\t- structured lesson records such as `repo_rule`, `workflow_rule`, `agent_instruction`, `skill_patch`, `anti_pattern`\n54\t\n55\tPrimary fields:\n56\t\n57\t- `summary`\n58\t- `failed_approaches[]`\n59\t- `error_signatures[]`\n60\t- `cause_chain[]`\n61\t- `file_dependencies[]`\n62\t- lower-signal context like `decisions[]`, `warnings[]`, `key_insights[]`\n63\t\n64\tPrimary purpose:\n65\t\n66\t- inject compact guidance into the next turn\n67\t\n68\tStrengths:\n69\t\n70\t- cheap to retrieve\n71\t- easy to score\n72\t- easy to inspect in TUI\n73\t- stable enough for review, suppression, promotion, and outcome tracking\n74\t\n75\tWeaknesses:\n76\t\n77\t- stores conclusions better than procedures\n78\t- does not preserve reusable solve paths well\n79\t- does not explicitly represent \"for this type of task, do A then B then C\"\n80\t- influences a combined agent through prompt injection rather than serving a distinct planner\n81\t\n82\t### MIA-style manager\n83\t\n84\tPrimary unit:\n85\t\n86\t- compressed task-solving trajectories and reusable experience\n87\t\n88\tPrimary purpose:\n89\t\n90\t- retrieve prior experience that helps determine how to solve a new task\n91\t\n92\tStrengths:\n93\t\n94\t- preserves procedural knowledge\n95\t- captures failed branches, recoveries, and ordering\n96\t- better fit for troubleshooting and multi-step task classes\n97\t\n98\tWeaknesses:\n99\t\n100\t- bulkier memory unit"}]},"uuid":"f8fabf8a-15d6-4cd8-9936-2a35aac5bd78","timestamp":"2026-04-28T21:34:47.175Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/alisha/.superset/worktrees/cli/agent-improvements/docs/plans/2026-04-16-experience-memory-layering-design.md","content":"# Experience Memory Layering Design\n\n## Summary\n\nThis document maps how to extend the current memory-loop system from lesson memory toward a more MIA-like manager without losing the advantages of the current rollout design.\n\nIt covers two paths:\n\n- a beta-compatible layered design that preserves the existing lesson-memory hot path and adds experience memory as a second retrieval layer\n- a longer-term experience-first redesign where episodes and playbooks become the primary memory unit and lessons become a derived product\n\nRecommendation:\n\n- ship the layered design first\n- keep lesson memory as the default hot path\n- add experience memory as procedural fallback\n- treat the experience-first design as post-beta architecture\n\n## Context\n\nThe current memory-loop rollout is designed as a compact guidance system:\n\n- fetch `CheckpointAnalysis`\n- generate structured memories\n- store and reconcile them\n- score them\n- inject the top matched memories into future sessions\n\nThat is valuable, but it is mostly \"remember the lesson.\"\n\nMIA's manager is closer to \"remember the way the task was solved.\" It stores compressed task-solving experience and retrieves it to help planning and execution. The gap is not that we lack memory entirely. The gap is that our memory is mostly declarative and cautionary, not procedural.\n\n## Goals\n\n- Preserve the current lesson-memory strengths: compactness, explainability, and fast injection.\n- Add procedural memory without blowing up token costs or retrieval latency.\n- Keep the beta rollout path incremental and shippable in small PRs.\n- Create a long-term architecture that can evolve toward planner-facing memory instead of prompt-only guidance.\n\n## Non-Goals\n\n- No attempt to reproduce MIA's reinforcement-learning setup.\n- No attempt to store full raw transcripts as the primary retrieval unit.\n- No beta requirement for a distinct planner service or separate execution runtime.\n- No immediate replacement of the current lesson-memory rollout.\n\n## Current Architecture vs MIA Manager\n\n### Current memory loop\n\nPrimary unit:\n\n- structured lesson records such as `repo_rule`, `workflow_rule`, `agent_instruction`, `skill_patch`, `anti_pattern`\n\nPrimary fields:\n\n- `summary`\n- `failed_approaches[]`\n- `error_signatures[]`\n- `cause_chain[]`\n- `file_dependencies[]`\n- lower-signal context like `decisions[]`, `warnings[]`, `key_insights[]`\n\nPrimary purpose:\n\n- inject compact guidance into the next turn\n\nStrengths:\n\n- cheap to retrieve\n- easy to score\n- easy to inspect in TUI\n- stable enough for review, suppression, promotion, and outcome tracking\n\nWeaknesses:\n\n- stores conclusions better than procedures\n- does not preserve reusable solve paths well\n- does not explicitly represent \"for this type of task, do A then B then C\"\n- influences a combined agent through prompt injection rather than serving a distinct planner\n\n### MIA-style manager\n\nPrimary unit:\n\n- compressed task-solving trajectories and reusable experience\n\nPrimary purpose:\n\n- retrieve prior experience that helps determine how to solve a new task\n\nStrengths:\n\n- preserves procedural knowledge\n- captures failed branches, recoveries, and ordering\n- better fit for troubleshooting and multi-step task classes\n\nWeaknesses:\n\n- bulkier memory unit","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":810}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8a153c5a-d269-4109-b57b-87eb0bbe941c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-cli","cwd":"/Users/alisha/.superset/worktrees/cli/agent-improvements","sessionId":"5eec25c8-61b1-4881-bbd6-91e8595e790c","version":"2.1.121","gitBranch":"agent-improvements"}
