{"parentUuid":"cfc7ee45-2e25-4772-8794-a759fb050750","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d3c558b8-5926-493c-a40f-61e97a9f4b9c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018ZXeLakwgN2144eAo4MpBs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Agent Memory: Lessons and Experiences\n2\t\n3\t## Summary\n4\t\n5\tAgent memory in the CLI is two distinct primitives stored side-by-side:\n6\t\n7\t- **Lessons** — compact declarative guidance (\"for this repo, always do X\") that injects into every eligible turn under a tight latency budget.\n8\t- **Experiences** — ordered procedural traces (\"when this class of task came up, here is how it got solved\") that inject only when the turn has a high procedural need.\n9\t\n10\tBoth primitives share a lifecycle, a scope model, a storage substrate, and a review surface. They differ in shape, generation path, retrieval path, and the signals that gate their injection. Lessons remain the hot path. Experiences are a second retrieval layer that earns its token cost only on tasks that resemble prior solve patterns.\n11\t\n12\tThis document starts from scratch. It replaces the separate lesson-first rollout (`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-07-memory-loop-rollout-design.md`) and experience-layering plan (`docs/plans/2026-04-16-experience-memory-layering-design.md`) with one unified architecture and one rollout path.\n13\t\n14\tTarget: external beta, opt-in. Once enabled, beta defaults to `mode=auto` and `policy=review`.\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t## 1. Goals and Non-Goals\n19\t\n20\t### Goals\n21\t\n22\t- One coherent memory system with two shapes (lessons and experiences), not two parallel memory systems bolted together.\n23\t- Preserve the current rollout's operational promises: fast injection, explainable behavior, governance via review.\n24\t- Add procedural memory without blowing the injection latency SLO or the token budget.\n25\t- Make suppression and dedup governance bulletproof: what a user suppresses stays suppressed, across extraction runs and analyzer version bumps.\n26\t- Treat LLM output as data, not instructions, at every boundary.\n27\t- Ship in small vertical slices. Each PR delivers one working feature end-to-end.\n28\t\n29\t### Non-Goals\n30\t\n31\t- No reinforcement-learning training loop.\n32\t- No replacement of `CheckpointAnalysis` as the primary analysis substrate. The entire.io API owns analysis; the CLI owns memory.\n33\t- No storage of raw transcripts in memory tables. Memory is compressed procedural or declarative structure, never a transcript excerpt.\n34\t- No separate planner service in beta. Memory is injected into the existing agent prompt path.\n35\t- No client-side trust of repo identity. Authorization lives on the API side.\n36\t\n37\t---\n38\t\n39\t## 2. Memory Model\n40\t\n41\t### The two primitives\n42\t\n43\t**Lesson** — a short structured rule derived from repeated friction or manually authored.\n44\t\n45\t- Kinds: `repo_rule`, `workflow_rule`, `agent_instruction`, `skill_patch`, `anti_pattern`\n46\t- Shape: `summary` + structured fields (`failed_approaches[]`, `error_signatures[]`, `cause_chain[]`, `file_dependencies[]`, etc.)\n47\t- Injection form: inline bullet in the turn prompt\n48\t- Answers: \"what should the agent remember?\"\n49\t\n50\t**Experience** — a compressed procedural trace of one task-solving run.\n51\t\n52\t- Shape: `goal_summary` + ordered `attempted_steps[]`, `successful_steps[]`, `failed_steps[]`, `tool_patterns[]`\n53\t- Injection form: a delimited `<prior-solve-path>` block when the turn has high procedural need\n54\t- Answers: \"how was a similar task solved before?\"\n55\t\n56\t### Relationship\n57\t\n58\tLessons and experiences are peers for storage, governance, and review. They are not peers at retrieval time:\n59\t\n60\t- Lessons are always eligible. Scoring runs every turn.\n61\t- Experiences are conditionally eligible. A procedural-need classifier gates retrieval.\n62\t- Lessons can be **derived** from repeated experiences under strict evidence-independence rules (Section 7).\n63\t- On conflict, lessons win. Experience injection is suppressed for a turn when an active lesson covers the same `task_class` or shares ≥50% of the experience's `file_dependencies`.\n64\t\n65\t### Lifecycle (shared)\n66\t\n67\tBoth memory types use the same lifecycle states and transitions:\n68\t\n69\t```\n70\t                activate / promote\n71\t          ┌──────────────────────────┐\n72\t          │                          ▼\n73\t    ┌───────────┐            ┌─────────────┐\n74\t    │ candidate │            │   active    │\n75\t    └───────────┘            └─────────────┘\n76\t          │                    │          │\n77\t          │ archive            │ suppress │ archive\n78\t          │                    ▼          │\n79\t          │              ┌────────────┐   │\n80\t          │              │ suppressed │   │\n81\t          │              └────────────┘   │\n82\t          │                    │          │\n83\t          │                    │ archive  │\n84\t          ▼                    ▼          ▼\n85\t    ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐\n86\t    │              archived                │\n87\t    └──────────────────────────────────────┘\n88\t```\n89\t\n90\t- `candidate` — generated but not eligible for injection\n91\t- `active` — eligible for injection\n92\t- `suppressed` — disabled by the user; fingerprint still blocks regeneration\n93\t- `archived` — permanently disabled, preserved for history only\n94\t\n95\t### Origin rules (shared)\n96\t\n97\t- `manual` — user-authored through the TUI. Lessons enter `active`. Experiences enter `candidate` so the rendered injection form can be reviewed once before it ships into sessions.\n98\t- `generated` — produced by the refresh pipeline. Follows activation policy:\n99\t  - `policy=auto` → enter `active`\n100\t  - `policy=review` → enter `candidate` (beta default)\n101\t- `generated_auto_promoted` — an experience auto-promoted by the quality-score seed in PR 10 (Section 10). Distinguished from human-promoted records for telemetry; still user-demotable.\n102\t- `derived` — a lesson synthesized from repeated experiences (Section 7). Always enters `candidate`.\n103\t\n104\t### Scopes (shared)\n105\t\n106\t- **Personal (`me`)** — keyed on `owner_id`, the GitHub username resolved via `gh auth status`. Repo-local only in beta.\n107\t- **Repo** — visible to all contributors through shared sync. Governed through review.\n108\t- **Branch** — ephemeral, tied to a git branch name. Auto-cleaned when the branch no longer exists in any worktree.\n109\t\n110\tScope promotion: `branch → me → repo` through an explicit promotion action in the TUI.\n111\t\n112\t### Modes and policies (shared)\n113\t\n114\t- `mode=off` — injection is a no-op. No scoring, no retrieval.\n115\t- `mode=auto` — matched memories are injected automatically.\n116\t- `policy=review` — generated memories enter as `candidate` (beta default).\n117\t- `policy=auto` — generated memories enter as `active`.\n118\t- `threshold=strict | balanced | flexible` — admission and scoring preset (beta default `balanced`).\n119\t\n120\t`manual` injection mode is out of scope for beta."}]},"uuid":"c75d376c-d7ae-440b-a94d-b74c40604601","timestamp":"2026-04-28T21:34:52.814Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/alisha/.superset/worktrees/cli/agent-improvements/docs/plans/2026-04-16-agent-memory-design.md","content":"# Agent Memory: Lessons and Experiences\n\n## Summary\n\nAgent memory in the CLI is two distinct primitives stored side-by-side:\n\n- **Lessons** — compact declarative guidance (\"for this repo, always do X\") that injects into every eligible turn under a tight latency budget.\n- **Experiences** — ordered procedural traces (\"when this class of task came up, here is how it got solved\") that inject only when the turn has a high procedural need.\n\nBoth primitives share a lifecycle, a scope model, a storage substrate, and a review surface. They differ in shape, generation path, retrieval path, and the signals that gate their injection. Lessons remain the hot path. Experiences are a second retrieval layer that earns its token cost only on tasks that resemble prior solve patterns.\n\nThis document starts from scratch. It replaces the separate lesson-first rollout (`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-04-07-memory-loop-rollout-design.md`) and experience-layering plan (`docs/plans/2026-04-16-experience-memory-layering-design.md`) with one unified architecture and one rollout path.\n\nTarget: external beta, opt-in. Once enabled, beta defaults to `mode=auto` and `policy=review`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Goals and Non-Goals\n\n### Goals\n\n- One coherent memory system with two shapes (lessons and experiences), not two parallel memory systems bolted together.\n- Preserve the current rollout's operational promises: fast injection, explainable behavior, governance via review.\n- Add procedural memory without blowing the injection latency SLO or the token budget.\n- Make suppression and dedup governance bulletproof: what a user suppresses stays suppressed, across extraction runs and analyzer version bumps.\n- Treat LLM output as data, not instructions, at every boundary.\n- Ship in small vertical slices. Each PR delivers one working feature end-to-end.\n\n### Non-Goals\n\n- No reinforcement-learning training loop.\n- No replacement of `CheckpointAnalysis` as the primary analysis substrate. The entire.io API owns analysis; the CLI owns memory.\n- No storage of raw transcripts in memory tables. Memory is compressed procedural or declarative structure, never a transcript excerpt.\n- No separate planner service in beta. Memory is injected into the existing agent prompt path.\n- No client-side trust of repo identity. Authorization lives on the API side.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Memory Model\n\n### The two primitives\n\n**Lesson** — a short structured rule derived from repeated friction or manually authored.\n\n- Kinds: `repo_rule`, `workflow_rule`, `agent_instruction`, `skill_patch`, `anti_pattern`\n- Shape: `summary` + structured fields (`failed_approaches[]`, `error_signatures[]`, `cause_chain[]`, `file_dependencies[]`, etc.)\n- Injection form: inline bullet in the turn prompt\n- Answers: \"what should the agent remember?\"\n\n**Experience** — a compressed procedural trace of one task-solving run.\n\n- Shape: `goal_summary` + ordered `attempted_steps[]`, `successful_steps[]`, `failed_steps[]`, `tool_patterns[]`\n- Injection form: a delimited `<prior-solve-path>` block when the turn has high procedural need\n- Answers: \"how was a similar task solved before?\"\n\n### Relationship\n\nLessons and experiences are peers for storage, governance, and review. They are not peers at retrieval time:\n\n- Lessons are always eligible. Scoring runs every turn.\n- Experiences are conditionally eligible. A procedural-need classifier gates retrieval.\n- Lessons can be **derived** from repeated experiences under strict evidence-independence rules (Section 7).\n- On conflict, lessons win. Experience injection is suppressed for a turn when an active lesson covers the same `task_class` or shares ≥50% of the experience's `file_dependencies`.\n\n### Lifecycle (shared)\n\nBoth memory types use the same lifecycle states and transitions:\n\n```\n                activate / promote\n          ┌──────────────────────────┐\n          │                          ▼\n    ┌───────────┐            ┌─────────────┐\n    │ candidate │            │   active    │\n    └───────────┘            └─────────────┘\n          │                    │          │\n          │ archive            │ suppress │ archive\n          │                    ▼          │\n          │              ┌────────────┐   │\n          │              │ suppressed │   │\n          │              └────────────┘   │\n          │                    │          │\n          │                    │ archive  │\n          ▼                    ▼          ▼\n    ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐\n    │              archived                │\n    └──────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n- `candidate` — generated but not eligible for injection\n- `active` — eligible for injection\n- `suppressed` — disabled by the user; fingerprint still blocks regeneration\n- `archived` — permanently disabled, preserved for history only\n\n### Origin rules (shared)\n\n- `manual` — user-authored through the TUI. Lessons enter `active`. Experiences enter `candidate` so the rendered injection form can be reviewed once before it ships into sessions.\n- `generated` — produced by the refresh pipeline. Follows activation policy:\n  - `policy=auto` → enter `active`\n  - `policy=review` → enter `candidate` (beta default)\n- `generated_auto_promoted` — an experience auto-promoted by the quality-score seed in PR 10 (Section 10). Distinguished from human-promoted records for telemetry; still user-demotable.\n- `derived` — a lesson synthesized from repeated experiences (Section 7). Always enters `candidate`.\n\n### Scopes (shared)\n\n- **Personal (`me`)** — keyed on `owner_id`, the GitHub username resolved via `gh auth status`. Repo-local only in beta.\n- **Repo** — visible to all contributors through shared sync. Governed through review.\n- **Branch** — ephemeral, tied to a git branch name. Auto-cleaned when the branch no longer exists in any worktree.\n\nScope promotion: `branch → me → repo` through an explicit promotion action in the TUI.\n\n### Modes and policies (shared)\n\n- `mode=off` — injection is a no-op. No scoring, no retrieval.\n- `mode=auto` — matched memories are injected automatically.\n- `policy=review` — generated memories enter as `candidate` (beta default).\n- `policy=auto` — generated memories enter as `active`.\n- `threshold=strict | balanced | flexible` — admission and scoring preset (beta default `balanced`).\n\n`manual` injection mode is out of scope for beta.","numLines":120,"startLine":1,"totalLines":751}},"sourceToolAssistant